==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 06 NOTES — fail2ban
Date: 20-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- fail2ban automates the Linux journal Day 28 manual "grep Failed password | count per IP" investigation
- Three numbers define a jail: maxretry (how many fails), findtime (time window), bantime (ban duration)
- Never edit jail.conf directly - overrides go in jail.local, which fail2ban merges on top
- A ban is enforced by inserting a real iptables rule (its own f2b-<jail> chain) automatically
- fail2ban-client status <jail> shows currently failed/banned counts and the ban list
- unbanip removes both the fail2ban-side ban record AND the underlying iptables rule

COMMANDS / ACTIONS I PRACTICED:
[ ] fail2ban-client status / status sshd
[ ] creating jail.local, enabling the sshd jail, restarting the service
[ ] deliberately failing SSH login 3 times to trigger a real ban on myself
[ ] checking the f2b-sshd iptables chain
[ ] reading /var/log/fail2ban.log for the actual ban decision
[ ] unbanning myself and confirming SSH access returned

paste or describe what you saw :
$ sudo fail2ban-client status sshd (after the ban)
> 

$ sudo iptables -L -n -v | grep -A 5 f2b-sshd
> 

$ sudo tail -20 /var/log/fail2ban.log (the ban line)
> 


